{
  "node_type": "statement",
  "body": {
    "id": "thm:weak-margin-equivalence",
    "kind": "theorem",
    "statement": "Whenever \\(\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(0)\\ne\\varnothing\\), for every nonempty stratum with \\(0\\le\\gamma\\le\\kappa_\\star/2\\), \\(R_{\\mathrm{or}}(\\gamma)\\asymp R_{\\mathrm{ad}}(\\gamma)\\asymp\\rho_{\\mathrm{ad}}(\\gamma)\\), uniformly in \\(T_0,T_1\\). Thus global honesty imposes no rate adaptation penalty throughout the weak-decoding half of the attainable margin range.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "T0",
      "T1",
      "kappaStar",
      "gamma",
      "Rad",
      "Ror",
      "rhoad"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "ass:margin",
      "def:adaptive-risk",
      "def:oracle-risk",
      "def:adaptive-rate",
      "thm:adaptive-frontier",
      "def:global-honesty",
      "def:oracle-honesty"
    ],
    "justification": "The theorem rules out the tempting but false claim that the unidentified boundary automatically creates an adaptation tax on every positive stratum.",
    "gap": "CaiLow2004 and NicklVanDeGeer2013 show that honesty may obstruct adaptation on other unions; the loading-margin union instead has an exact no-penalty region that requires same-stratum compensated rotations.",
    "consumer": "It tells users with weak estimated decoding geometry that supplying an external lower margin bound cannot improve the achievable order of interval width.",
    "proof_tex": "Because \\(\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(\\gamma)\\subseteq\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(0)\\), every globally honest procedure is \\(\\gamma\\)-honest.  Definitions \\texttt{def:global-honesty}, \\texttt{def:oracle-honesty}, \\texttt{def:adaptive-risk}, and \\texttt{def:oracle-risk} therefore give\n\\[\n R_{\\mathrm{or}}(\\gamma)\\le R_{\\mathrm{ad}}(\\gamma)\n \\le C\\rho_{\\mathrm{ad}}(\\gamma)\n\\]\nby the proved \\texttt{thm:adaptive-frontier}.  It remains to obtain the same-stratum lower bound.\n\nThe direct post-noise pair and the fixed-geometry weak-decoding pair in the proof of \\texttt{thm:adaptive-frontier} already put both parameter points in \\(\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(\\gamma)\\).  They supply, respectively,\n\\[\n \\min\\{B,T_1^{-1/2}\\},\n \\qquad\n \\min\\{B,A,T_1^{-1/2}/\\gamma\\}.\n\\]\nFor the pre-panel term, suppose \\(0<\\gamma\\le\\kappa_\\star/2\\).  The attained-range argument in that proof supplies a loading space \\(L_0\\) with \\(\\kappa_s(L_0)=2\\gamma\\) and a unit \\(2s\\)-sparse minimizer \\(h\\).  Repeat the compensated-rotation construction with \\(2\\gamma\\) in place of \\(\\gamma\\), choosing\n\\[\n t=c\\min\\{B,A,AT_0^{-1/2}/\\gamma\\}\n\\]\nfor a sufficiently small fixed \\(c\\).  Its full-observation Gaussian mean distance is bounded, so the same elementary total-variation calculation applies.\n\nIt remains only to verify membership of the alternative in the oracle stratum.  For arbitrary rank-\\(r\\) projectors \\(Q,Q'\\) and every unit \\(2s\\)-sparse \\(x\\),\n\\[\n \\left|\\lVert(I_N-Q)x\\rVert_2-\\lVert(I_N-Q')x\\rVert_2\\right|\n \\le\\lVert(Q-Q')x\\rVert_2\n \\le\\lVert Q-Q'\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}.\n\\]\nTaking minima proves\n\\[\n |\\kappa_s(\\operatorname{range}Q)-\\kappa_s(\\operatorname{range}Q')|\n \\le\\lVert Q-Q'\\rVert_{\\mathrm{op}}.\n\\]\nIn the rotation construction, projector distance equals the sine of the rotation angle and is at most a fixed multiple of \\(t(2\\gamma)/A\\).  The constant \\(c\\) can be chosen, independently of \\(T_0,T_1,\\gamma\\), so that this distance is at most \\(\\gamma\\).  Therefore the rotated alternative has margin at least \\(2\\gamma-\\gamma=\\gamma\\), and both laws used in the lower bound belong to \\(\\mathcal M_{T_0,T_1}(\\gamma)\\).  This supplies\n\\[\n \\min\\{B,A,AT_0^{-1/2}/\\gamma\\}.\n\\]\nThe same three-term comparison used in \\texttt{thm:adaptive-frontier} yields \\(R_{\\mathrm{or}}(\\gamma)\\ge c\\rho_{\\mathrm{ad}}(\\gamma)\\).  At \\(\\gamma=0\\), the two model classes and the two honesty classes coincide by definition, so \\(R_{\\mathrm{or}}(0)=R_{\\mathrm{ad}}(0)\\).  Combining the bounds proves the asserted uniform equivalence throughout \\(0\\le\\gamma\\le\\kappa_\\star/2\\), including unequal sample sizes and all saturation regimes.",
